⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1)⟩\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104140/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed6fe6c9-4c20-5481-a63f-ff9121824e94"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104140"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection/50091e1ab7bc8aa2b187a85174b321e771858988b2740cca6c2a8e3b180fb381" :answer/id #uuid "a123db7e-45b9-5e2e-96af-60bded010298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}v-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection/51bd175d7f4fd2d792c6cc80af1e1b562ccc0c235e69bd338a4f2a21ebc1af81" :answer/id #uuid "dec0b316-ba93-5624-b046-41ef79d515d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{u-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection/9ef63b7219412bf602ddf1ee5ebc32aa922dd2610a311897bb6c993a06552e87" :answer/id #uuid "88ab374b-7c10-5bc6-b3db-64e594cddd5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{uv-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection/fb9295ddcdb6a9c6fd093f2a2cab5afc0c08f35fdf515dd184fe9facf49abd11" :answer/id #uuid "2e1be00c-efda-5c47-8544-37d401ce1b9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection/86e068ad92f22f3da0a1ee63054380ad7ae21a11e5e26a9a946323a26a9f2b0a" :answer/id #uuid "b17b86ae-9653-528d-84f5-81dadceec85b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3uv}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce7a0ffd-6053-50c8-8065-a31a7c9b5ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104181/selection/fb9295ddcdb6a9c6fd093f2a2cab5afc0c08f35fdf515dd184fe9facf49abd11" :answer-field/choices ["q-104181/selection/50091e1ab7bc8aa2b187a85174b321e771858988b2740cca6c2a8e3b180fb381" "q-104181/selection/51bd175d7f4fd2d792c6cc80af1e1b562ccc0c235e69bd338a4f2a21ebc1af81" "q-104181/selection/9ef63b7219412bf602ddf1ee5ebc32aa922dd2610a311897bb6c993a06552e87" "q-104181/selection/fb9295ddcdb6a9c6fd093f2a2cab5afc0c08f35fdf515dd184fe9facf49abd11" "q-104181/selection/86e068ad92f22f3da0a1ee63054380ad7ae21a11e5e26a9a946323a26a9f2b0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104181" :question/id #uuid "d304d1bf-04b6-5fa3-8eec-a0e6476429ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-104181" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}y-{z}^{3}=3.$ \n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-{z}^{3} & =3 \\\\ {z}^{3} & ={x}^{2}y-3 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}y-3}\\,k.\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}v-3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104181/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104181"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection/bf6c3c41e7205934b802122fe0023c9b9d1acc1827a6fe888ea45d79dd34c83c" :answer/id #uuid "f8df0ad3-ef65-53ea-9361-b77bb8ff853f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3v+2u)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection/cb7e67e177308a897ce1aafb46b2435e052a20497f543f485af36c9ae8f2ddc4" :answer/id #uuid "13296ee7-6bce-54e9-a8d3-d4128344429f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3v-2u)^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection/220daa748cc5dafffe96a3b1844dc3ad25e7f1761f5461daf9e3f952878b43ef" :answer/id #uuid "272c698f-b7bf-5a67-87a0-7f316a861c8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(-3v+2u)^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection/f3c987393419518991140fc8f0ff0295aa772d3b11c5dfad37029bc4105362d0" :answer/id #uuid "557309a4-9929-5f93-a122-3331f09ce821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(-3v+2u)^{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection/c36a2a6c2b828f602aa1b51e3cd9fe521198dad26060f9b4e7dd5cca3524f3d0" :answer/id #uuid "41d85f53-3254-55f8-91c7-f40bc557d750" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2ea5e2-8a2c-5178-a3f8-24e149094b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104182/selection/c36a2a6c2b828f602aa1b51e3cd9fe521198dad26060f9b4e7dd5cca3524f3d0" :answer-field/choices ["q-104182/selection/bf6c3c41e7205934b802122fe0023c9b9d1acc1827a6fe888ea45d79dd34c83c" "q-104182/selection/cb7e67e177308a897ce1aafb46b2435e052a20497f543f485af36c9ae8f2ddc4" "q-104182/selection/220daa748cc5dafffe96a3b1844dc3ad25e7f1761f5461daf9e3f952878b43ef" "q-104182/selection/f3c987393419518991140fc8f0ff0295aa772d3b11c5dfad37029bc4105362d0" "q-104182/selection/c36a2a6c2b828f602aa1b51e3cd9fe521198dad26060f9b4e7dd5cca3524f3d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104182" :question/id #uuid "8e0e0f80-cd50-5f5b-a6b4-c3eb06298d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-104182" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+\\sqrt[3]{2z} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2z} & =3y-2x \\\\ z & =\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\frac{(3y-2x)^{3}}{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{(3v-2u)^{3}}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104182/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104182"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection/c3219f73d8d892a818112585742c5d0634aa147ab119a1dba84acfda1bb8d4b1" :answer/id #uuid "f19bd09b-ead0-5950-b159-2999152b5731" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(u+v)^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection/44a4f78e578da100c7e98f0f32d7238eafe006bdad713081d0910d17a4b442ad" :answer/id #uuid "d5fe475d-c261-590f-97f5-0d43bbda476d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection/ed7054e085d8db94ac577856bbcb2f379371e457568bd65f0138030c6938ee99" :answer/id #uuid "4082770e-e194-5538-b403-21e32d71647d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{(u+v)^{3}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection/480606d87ba0162decf524801ecee0bd3261ed6af68801f097803e507b104674" :answer/id #uuid "812315f7-e33b-58f1-bd1f-9a263996b291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection/8c7bd9e731be3dfc41c6ff9813c4c43c1f9680560c73700e953ed999ed2874ec" :answer/id #uuid "7b8a3e14-f471-5190-bced-8ee99352ad5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cdd6da0-a594-5eb9-b3cd-5276f69da0af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104170/selection/480606d87ba0162decf524801ecee0bd3261ed6af68801f097803e507b104674" :answer-field/choices ["q-104170/selection/c3219f73d8d892a818112585742c5d0634aa147ab119a1dba84acfda1bb8d4b1" "q-104170/selection/44a4f78e578da100c7e98f0f32d7238eafe006bdad713081d0910d17a4b442ad" "q-104170/selection/ed7054e085d8db94ac577856bbcb2f379371e457568bd65f0138030c6938ee99" "q-104170/selection/480606d87ba0162decf524801ecee0bd3261ed6af68801f097803e507b104674" "q-104170/selection/8c7bd9e731be3dfc41c6ff9813c4c43c1f9680560c73700e953ed999ed2874ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104170" :question/id #uuid "6d37951d-f75b-50a7-a043-80a7f31b132e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104170" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104170/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104170"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection/bbb8dd9c5ea42e75786720e81b5951da1e21c484726057657acb29a18b3ac622" :answer/id #uuid "cc2a5195-90bc-5cd7-a852-77c0303955e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection/6e175789da5d20f7d371aba40447a73611c0130ac4bfff47c8ba9e0f1ed02264" :answer/id #uuid "b6df7fa4-1196-5207-95e7-52b0cd7c693e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection/b5d8e5b2e9182e8c562a397c88e22562be4d41fbec160f433709874d90b4eaa5" :answer/id #uuid "bac673aa-51f7-556c-8971-0df4408a1932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection/5e0f6c48ca0aca44d7997224312dabbd4fac5b6b2b0cee85ea9aa6519aeabcb9" :answer/id #uuid "d56267fe-8124-581f-a027-3ef93fde911e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection/39625942b24c3b1362da43bf5a873ad6d54eb7926baa3adf1ca0d7e9269e1079" :answer/id #uuid "5428c067-f0a9-5173-b055-49df0d695d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "859c53b1-1fd6-50b7-937a-8d6cd49d6532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104172/selection/6e175789da5d20f7d371aba40447a73611c0130ac4bfff47c8ba9e0f1ed02264" :answer-field/choices ["q-104172/selection/bbb8dd9c5ea42e75786720e81b5951da1e21c484726057657acb29a18b3ac622" "q-104172/selection/6e175789da5d20f7d371aba40447a73611c0130ac4bfff47c8ba9e0f1ed02264" "q-104172/selection/b5d8e5b2e9182e8c562a397c88e22562be4d41fbec160f433709874d90b4eaa5" "q-104172/selection/5e0f6c48ca0aca44d7997224312dabbd4fac5b6b2b0cee85ea9aa6519aeabcb9" "q-104172/selection/39625942b24c3b1362da43bf5a873ad6d54eb7926baa3adf1ca0d7e9269e1079"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104172" :question/id #uuid "1d57744a-d330-5f8c-832f-b2905e398bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104172" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104172/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104172"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-103787"] :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=2$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2\\ge 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-103789"] :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection/a776c264a990bf68bb2034eaeca84e0ebdd6ce793d1fc60e9c650d85d1d1add6" :answer/id #uuid "519b8e23-7e98-58fb-a872-2349d1055a67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection/f2fc1361a33c721eeda11e3149a97e442c51d9d353a7cd108aaecb666d3c69e2" :answer/id #uuid "d4ada32b-7b7d-59c5-a767-4362f1b5f845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection/f8704639cac47b28adff85885a40e6acd9c6c10deab63529354a3e3413db726f" :answer/id #uuid "b073fa80-ae0a-5722-b190-a1777cc06ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" :answer/id #uuid "659db62e-df43-5ca5-af17-4e0a2e45ca31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection/961e6c26a3930850ad6ac8329a88a3928f75499547a7df98c65a4cdca366e99a" :answer/id #uuid "ce6588ac-c52b-5c95-b7ee-770b38121126" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "93a045dd-59cc-593b-a258-8991a62630ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104273/selection/f8704639cac47b28adff85885a40e6acd9c6c10deab63529354a3e3413db726f" :answer-field/choices ["q-104273/selection/a776c264a990bf68bb2034eaeca84e0ebdd6ce793d1fc60e9c650d85d1d1add6" "q-104273/selection/f2fc1361a33c721eeda11e3149a97e442c51d9d353a7cd108aaecb666d3c69e2" "q-104273/selection/f8704639cac47b28adff85885a40e6acd9c6c10deab63529354a3e3413db726f" "q-104273/selection/3241760d793c1deb77d5b8fed071cd166050543adeae163ee6445a08f221d6dc" "q-104273/selection/961e6c26a3930850ad6ac8329a88a3928f75499547a7df98c65a4cdca366e99a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104273" :question/id #uuid "51f7a8a6-a740-5f12-9a11-a82ea66f3f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-104273" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104273/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c"] :knowl